# Target C — star-center articulation frontier

**LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN.**

This module records C60 from
`docs/reports/target_c_zero_transfer_star_center_articulation_2026-08-31.md`.
It sharpens C58's zero-transfer star forest and feeds C59's collinear multi-body branch.

## C60 resistance-equality theorem

In any finite connected positive-conductance graph,

`R_ab = R_ac + R_cb`

holds exactly when deleting `c` separates `a` from `b`.

Proof: ground `c`, inject one unit from `a` to `c`, and let `phi` be the voltage. The
quadratic resistance identity gives

`R_ab = R_ac + R_bc - 2 phi_b`.

The strong discrete maximum principle says `phi` is strictly positive on the component
of `G-c` containing `a` and identically zero on every other component. Equality is
therefore equivalent to separation by `c`.

## Zero-transfer consequence

For a C56-Z diameter star with center `o` and leaves `v_i`, C58 gives

`R_vi,vj = R_vi,o + R_o,vj`.

Hence `o` itself is the source positive-contact articulation separating every two
diameter leaves. In particular

`deg_contact(o) >= deg_diameter_star(o)`.

The triangle-free unit-contact degree cap immediately gives diameter-star degree at
most five. Unlike SEPq, this identifies the separator and applies already to a
two-leaf star.

## Null-body consequence

C59 proves that two differently rotating extreme null bodies meet at one source hinge
and their finite relative rotation is feasible. Their midpoint positive support is
literally a graph one-sum at that hinge. If a midpoint diameter star contains edges
from both bodies, its center is the same hinge and C60 identifies it as the contact
articulation.

For three differently rotating null bodies, every positive convex mixture of the
three extreme stresses is itself C56-Z: if it were C56-G, the common selected diameter
angular rate would force all three body rates equal. Therefore the complete three-body
diameter union is a degree-at-most-five star forest, and every nontrivial star center
is a contact articulation of the complete three-body positive support.

C59 independently forces the three distinct pairwise hinge vertices of a nontrivial
three-body motion to be collinear.

## Next exact target

Exclude the resulting collinear pin cycle, or integrate it. The remaining covered
three-body obstruction is:

- three rigid full-diameter null bodies;
- pairwise one-vertex intersections at three distinct collinear source points;
- complete positive diameter union a bounded star forest;
- every nontrivial diameter-star center an articulation of the full mixed positive
  contact graph.

After that, the independent issue is whether the null-face body cover contains every
active vertex needed for the finite release.
